WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.418 --> 00:00:04.463
(dramatic strings playing)

00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:14.348
This episode switches perspective

00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:18.018
from our central trio
of Charles, Oliver, and Mabel

00:00:18.101 --> 00:00:19.853
&lt;i&gt;to Theo Dimas.&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:19.937 --> 00:00:21.813
John pitched me the episode

00:00:21.897 --> 00:00:23.690
&lt;i&gt;as this silent episode&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:23.774 --> 00:00:25.817
&lt;i&gt;entirely from the point of view&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;of the deaf character.&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:30.364
One of the things we were
really adamant about was this

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:31.698
&lt;i&gt;was not just a gimmick.&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:31.782 --> 00:00:33.951
&lt;i&gt;The person driving the episode&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;is a central,&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:36.411
emotional component
of the overall season.

00:00:46.255 --> 00:00:49.049
I had the great privilege
of meeting James Caverly

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:51.593
&lt;i&gt;who plays Theo early on in prep.&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:54.471
&lt;i&gt;And it was great to meet him&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;because he asked me some great questions&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:55.806
&lt;i&gt;and one of those questions was,&lt;/i&gt;

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.183
how will the deaf audience
know the difference

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:02.020
between Theo's storyline and
the storyline of the hearing characters.

00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:19.162
It's great to inform the way
in which I wanted to shoot the episode.

00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:21.456
&lt;i&gt;We did a lot of&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;point-of-view shots&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.333
and we were really, really committed to

00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.878
whatever Theo sees,
that's what we're shooting.

00:01:25.961 --> 00:01:28.839
&lt;i&gt;We're really grounding it in&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;his subjective point of view.&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:34.720
JOHN HOFFMAN:&lt;i&gt; My favorite scene&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;is the crux of the episode&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:38.390
where Theo and Zoe
are on the rooftop.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:42.060
CHERIEN DABIS:&lt;i&gt; This is the moment that&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;we've been waiting for the entire series&lt;/i&gt;

00:01:42.144 --> 00:01:45.105
where we finally get to find out
what happened 10 years ago.

00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:39.076
I just felt stunned
by the level of performance

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:41.286
&lt;i&gt;from Olivia and James.&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.288
Working with James was so much fun

00:02:43.372 --> 00:02:45.207
in a way I've never experienced before.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.335
&lt;i&gt;We met on Zoom for some rehearsal&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;because I had realized that&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:52.464
&lt;i&gt;I wouldn't know what my cues were&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;because his lines were also in sign.&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:52.548 --> 00:02:55.926
Nathan and Olivia
learned ASL for this

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:57.511
and did such a remarkable job.

00:03:09.356 --> 00:03:13.026
It's very challenging but Doug
coached me through the whole thing.

00:03:15.988 --> 00:03:18.657
And of course James Caverly
was very supportive

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.201
&lt;i&gt;of me trying to speak his language.&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:04.119
He's an extraordinary young actor

00:04:04.203 --> 00:04:07.247
&lt;i&gt;and his ability to do something&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;that was very difficult and very emotional&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:07.331 --> 00:04:09.208
&lt;i&gt;take after take after take,&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:09.291 --> 00:04:10.918
&lt;i&gt;I was so proud of him.&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:11.001 --> 00:04:13.378
It was just one of those moments
where you get goosebumps

00:04:13.462 --> 00:04:15.130
and remember
how much you love your job

00:04:15.214 --> 00:04:17.090
because you're like
the first audience of something.

00:04:34.024 --> 00:04:35.817
DABIS:
As an Arab-American woman,

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:38.904
I feel like so much of my career
has been about representing

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:40.781
&lt;i&gt;the underrepresented,&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:40.864 --> 00:04:42.658
&lt;i&gt;and to be able to do that now&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;in the deaf community,&lt;/i&gt;

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:44.451
&lt;i&gt;I just was so honored.&lt;/i&gt;

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:02.302
OLIVIA REIS:&lt;i&gt; I think it&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;opens up a broader world&lt;/i&gt;

00:05:02.386 --> 00:05:04.721
for what we expect
from television.

00:05:04.805 --> 00:05:07.015
I think the bar is being raised
because we're seeing

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:09.518
that this works
really, really well.

00:05:29.496 --> 00:05:32.124
♪ ♪

